ack on its agenda of  delivering levelling up because i think  that's the thing that will really swing  people's vote of the next general  election yeah covet has been seen as a  distraction from the leveling up agenda  we're still going through a pandemic but  as you said eyes are now turning to  those uh those promises and talk to me  about what leveling up means then for  the wider area you're talking about  outside of t side because at the moment  there is a cost of living crisis for  example would it mean more to people in  the north to have help to pay  their heating bills than it would be to  say get a new train station  well i think leveling up actually helps  deliver on some of those uh issues that  we're facing today so actually leveling  up on for me my personal opinion on  phase one is around large infrastructure  because it's infrastructure that large  parts of the north of england haven't  had for decades and the governments of  both colours that's better roads it's  better rail it's more bus services it's  even infrastructure that will help  enable private sector investment and if  you look for specifically at the cost of  living in particular around energy  pricing there's more that could be done  by the government and people like off  gen to be able to approve more energy  production more gas production within  the uk that it currently isn't and  actually a lot of that would take place  in places like the north of england  creating many thousands of jobs for  people in good well-paid sectors so all  of this comes together phase one is  about getting large parts of the north  of england to the starting line because  there are places including like t-side  that don't have a metro system we don't  have a regular bus system we don't have  the fantastic infrastructure that other  cities and parts of the south have and  then it allows us to give back to uk plc  and that will by definition address some  of those cost of living issues that  you've just raised yeah people's pockets  being squeezed but the government would  argue that their pockets are squeezed as  well do you think that the government  can level up and cut taxes  and this is a really interesting  argument about levelling up because  people assume if you're in favor of  leveling up you're therefore in favor of  just spending huge sums of money  irrespective of from what may and  actually it's a bit of a balance there  is going to be investment needed from  the government when it talks about  public infrastructure those things that  i've just talked about around rail and  road and buses etc making sure that  people can connect to those  opportunities for jobs and investment  but actually enabling infrastructure  will give more confidence to the private  sector and international corporates and  money from across the world to come and  invest in the uk that's private money  coming in that's leveraging the money  that the government uh can put in it  creates jobs that means more tax take it  means that we're getting more businesses  setting up in the uk so leveling up is  not mutually exclusive from being able  to try and keep taxes low and  incentivize the private sector those two  things go hand in hand and t-side's a  great example of that right we we are  the architect of one of the most free  market policies that this government has  come up with in free parts that doesn't  require hard cash it requires a  framework that enables the private  sector which they're already doing to  come and invest money and create jobs  cct side very much as a blueprint for  perhaps those areas of the north that  aren't benefiting from the leveling up  agenda yet there's quite a gap isn't  there between now and the 2019 general  election if you look at what labour is  saying they're sort of looking towards a  general election in the summer of next  year that's not a lot of time for boris  johnson to prove to northern voters that  he's serious about leveling up what can  